        "id": "p25-node-02-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$y=2x-1$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "(IV): $(-\\infty,\\infty)$.",
          "worked": "The nonhorizontal straight line extends without bound upwards and downwards, reaching every real $y$-value. Its range is $(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nRange IV.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-node-02-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$y=\\sqrt{x}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "(I): $[0,\\infty)$.",
          "worked": "Square-root outputs are nonnegative. At $x=0$, $y=0$, and the square-root graph reaches every positive height. Its range is $[0,\\infty)$.\n\nRange I.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-node-02-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$y=x^2$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "(I): $[0,\\infty)$.",
          "worked": "$x^2\\geq0$, with equality at $x=0$. The upward-opening parabola reaches every height above its minimum of $0$, so its range is $[0,\\infty)$.\n\nRange I.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-node-02-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$y=x^3$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "(IV): $(-\\infty,\\infty)$.",
          "worked": "The continuous cubic graph extends without bound downwards and upwards, reaching every real $y$-value. Its range is $(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nRange IV.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-node-02-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$y=\\frac{1}{x}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "(III): $(-\\infty,0)\\cup(0,\\infty)$.",
          "worked": "A reciprocal can take any positive or negative value, but it cannot equal zero. Its range is $(-\\infty,0)\\cup(0,\\infty)$.\n\nRange III.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-node-02-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "$y=\\frac{1}{x^2}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "(II): $(0,\\infty)$.",
          "worked": "The denominator requires $x\\ne0$, so $x^2>0$ and $\\frac{1}{x^2}>0$. The graph reaches every positive height, approaching zero without attaining it and extending without bound upwards near $x=0$. Its range is $(0,\\infty)$.\n\nRange II.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 0
      }
    ]
